            tagline = "A forgotten code on a community garden gate leads students to the people who built it.",
            parts = listOf(
                StoryPart(1, "The Numbers on the Gate", listOf(
                    "A small community garden stood between the library and the bus road. Its gate had a keypad, but the code had been removed from the public notice after the volunteer group changed. When Fara joined the school's service club, she asked why the garden was locked even during advertised opening hours. The library manager explained that the garden committee was rebuilding its volunteer rota and would publish access arrangements when ready.",
                    "Fara noticed four faded numbers scratched into a wooden sign beside the gate. Her friend Emeka thought they might be the old code, but their teacher reminded them not to try combinations or enter without permission. Instead, they asked the library manager whether the sign had historical value. She agreed to find the garden's public records."
                )),
                StoryPart(2, "The First Committee", listOf(
                    "The records showed that the garden had been created by a group of residents who wanted a shared outdoor space. The first committee included teachers, shopkeepers, and library volunteers. The keypad had been installed years later to protect tools after several items went missing. The numbers on the sign were not a code at all; they were the year the garden opened, written in a decorative style.",
                    "Fara and Emeka found a photograph of the opening day. Behind the gate stood a group holding a banner that read EVERYONE HELPS IT GROW. They recognised the library manager's father in the picture. She said he had helped build the first raised beds and might still have the original planting plan."
                )),
                StoryPart(3, "A Plan in a Folder", listOf(
                    "The planting plan was stored in a folder at the library. It showed paths, seating, water points, and a section reserved for accessible beds. The current garden had changed, but the plan explained why the paths were wider near the entrance. Fara suggested comparing the old layout with the garden's present needs before proposing any changes.",
                    "The manager invited the current committee to meet the students. They explained that the garden had become difficult to maintain because volunteers had different schedules. The gate was not meant to exclude neighbours; it was there to protect equipment and ensure that someone was present during opening hours. The students offered to help create a clearer public timetable."
                )),
                StoryPart(4, "The Keyholder", listOf(
                    "The committee introduced Fara to Mr Ojo, the volunteer who held the authorised access key. He described how the garden's opening times were agreed and why the keypad code was shared only with registered volunteers. He said the committee wanted visitors to enjoy the space safely, but it needed a reliable way to know who was responsible for tools and maintenance.",
                    "Fara proposed a public sign with opening hours, contact details for the library desk, and a note explaining how to join a supervised gardening session. The committee liked the idea but asked that the sign not display private volunteer numbers. Emeka drafted a version using the library's public contact channel."
                )),
                StoryPart(5, "The Empty Bed", listOf(
                    "During a supervised visit, the students noticed one raised bed was empty. The old plan labelled it as a learning bed where children could grow plants from seed. The committee said it had been left unused because no one had been available to lead the sessions. Fara suggested asking the school science club whether a teacher could supervise a short project.",
                    "The science teacher agreed to help plan a seasonal activity, provided the committee approved the plants and schedule. The students researched suitable options from trusted gardening references and checked the garden's sunlight and water conditions with the committee. They chose a small, manageable project rather than promising a full harvest."
                )),
                StoryPart(6, "A Welcome Sign", listOf(
                    "The students designed a sign with simple symbols, large lettering, and a clear explanation of supervised sessions. They included a reminder to keep paths clear and to ask the library desk about access. The committee reviewed the wording and corrected the opening hours before sending it to print.",
                    "One volunteer suggested adding the names of everyone who had ever helped. The committee decided that a full list would be difficult to verify, so they chose to thank the original committee and all later volunteers collectively. The old opening photograph would be displayed inside the library with a caption identifying only people whose names were confirmed."
                )),
                StoryPart(7, "A Day of Small Jobs", listOf(
                    "On the first supervised workday, volunteers checked the raised beds, cleaned labels, and recorded which tools needed repair. Students were assigned simple tasks with adult guidance. Nobody changed the gate system or handled equipment without permission. Fara helped update the planting plan so future volunteers could see what had been checked and what still needed attention.",
                    "The committee discovered that the water schedule had been copied incorrectly on an old notice. They corrected it and asked the library to display the new version. Emeka suggested a sign-in sheet for supervised sessions, and the committee adopted one that collected only the information needed for safety and organisation."
                )),
                StoryPart(8, "The Opening Hours", listOf(
                    "The garden reopened for two weekly public sessions, with a committee volunteer present. The library posted the timetable and explained how residents could ask about joining. Families visited, read the garden history, and helped with the learning bed. The keypad remained in place for tools and out-of-hours security; the public did not need its code to take part.",
                    "Fara noticed that people were more comfortable when they knew when someone would be there. A parent asked whether the garden could host a reading afternoon. The committee agreed to consider it after the first month, once the volunteer rota was stable. The garden was becoming welcoming through clear arrangements, not by removing every boundary."
                )),
                StoryPart(9, "The Original Builders", listOf(
                    "The library hosted a small display with the opening photograph, the original plan, and the new timetable. Mr Ojo spoke about the residents who had built the first beds. The library manager added her father's name after checking it with the family. Fara's club presented the updated sign and the record of the learning bed.",
                    "Several former volunteers came forward with memories and photographs. The committee added verified material to the archive and invited people to submit corrections through the library. They did not publish private contact details or unconfirmed stories. The students learned that community history grows best when it can be checked and shared respectfully."
                )),
                StoryPart(10, "A Gate That Welcomes", listOf(
                    "At the end of term, the garden held its first open reading afternoon. Children read beneath the shade while volunteers watered the learning bed. The new sign showed the public sessions, and the library desk helped visitors understand how to join. The gate was still locked outside supervised hours, but it no longer felt like a mystery.",
                    "Fara returned to the faded numbers on the old wooden sign. They marked the garden's opening year, not a secret entrance. The real key had been the committee's willingness to explain how the space worked and invite new helpers into it. The garden was open in the way that mattered: people knew when they could come, how to participate, and who was caring for the place."
                ))
            )
